#! The contextual `error` (s158, wolf-lang#36; wolf-lsp#22), through the ONE
#! surface in this repository that can paint it: the server's semantic-token
#! walk. `error` is contextual by a wider margin than `then` — wolfc decides
#! it on THREE tokens, `Ident("error")`, an `IDENT`, then `=` — so a TextMate
#! `match`, a `syn keyword` and an emacs word list cannot approximate the test
#! at all (tl04 classified it `CONTEXTUAL`; clients/vscode/inventory.md), and
#! `rows/error_alias_ident.lu` spends its whole length on what guessing costs:
#! a FIELD, a FUNCTION, a BINDING and two expression uses, all spelled `error`
#! and none of them the keyword.
#!
#! Two documents, so the transcript carries both halves of the claim.
#! `rows/error_alias_union.lu` has TWO `error` items (the second names the
#! first inside its row) and puts the alias in ROW position and in the
#! brace-less TYPE tail `-> int ! ConfigErrors`; the whole document first,
#! then each item's line alone as a `/range` request. `rows/error_alias_ident.lu`
#! is the negative witness: every `error` in it must keep its identifier type.
#!
#! WHAT IT MEASURED, at pin 30731a6 (v0.2.14), the first pin whose server
#! paints a contextual keyword at all (wolf-lang `928f9d9`, the fix for #356
#! that closed the `then` hole — and, as its author said, the `error` hole
#! beside it, "never measured"). This is the measurement. wolf-lsp#22 named
#! three outcomes — `keyword`, some other type, or nothing — and it is the
#! FIRST:
#!
#!   union L12 C0   `error`         keyword
#!   union L12 C6   `IoErrors`      type  declaration
#!   union L14 C0   `error`         keyword
#!   union L14 C6   `ConfigErrors`  type  declaration
#!
#! 24 tokens for the document; the two `/range` requests answer exactly the
#! two tokens of their line each, so it is not a full-document artifact. And
#! the negative half holds — the walk is not matching the word:
#!
#!   ident L12 C4   `error`  property  declaration   (the field)
#!   ident L15 C3   `error`  function  declaration   (the function)
#!   ident L20 C8   `error`  variable  declaration,readonly   (the binding)
#!   ident L21 C12  `error`  variable  readonly      (interpolated)
#!   ident L22 C21  `error`  property                (the field init)
#!   ident L22 C28  `error`  variable  readonly      (the initializer)
#!   ident L23 C14  `error`  property                (the field read)
#!
#! 26 tokens, and not one of the seven is `keyword`. So #11 and #22 are NOT
#! one bug with two witnesses at this pin: they were at v0.2.12, and one
#! upstream commit closed both.
#!
#! ONE HOLE REMAINS, and it is a different one, filed rather than re-recorded:
#! the alias NAME is painted `type` where it is DECLARED and painted NOTHING
#! where it is USED. `IoErrors` inside `{IoErrors, closed}` (L14 C22) and
#! `ConfigErrors` in `-> int ! ConfigErrors` (L16 C27) are both absent from
#! the stream, while the `bool` (L16 C12) and `int` (L16 C21) beside them are
#! `type`. Row TAGS (`none`, `parse`, `closed`, and `return closed`) were never
#! tokenized at any pin and are not claimed here; the alias name is new at
#! this pin and is the thing that reads inconsistently. Reported upstream
#! with this stream; this transcript pins the current answer so the fix
#! announces itself as a red here.
name      annotate/semanticTokens-error
profile   minimal

initialize
open      rows/error_alias_union.lu
wait      diagnostics rows/error_alias_union.lu
req       semanticTokens rows/error_alias_union.lu
req       semanticTokens rows/error_alias_union.lu 11:0-12:0
req       semanticTokens rows/error_alias_union.lu 13:0-14:0
open      rows/error_alias_ident.lu
wait      diagnostics rows/error_alias_ident.lu
req       semanticTokens rows/error_alias_ident.lu
shutdown
